body:
🔴 **Bug**: `dailyEmailTracker` grows without bound. Unlike `dailyIpTracker` which filters old entries on read (line 33), the email tracker only stores the last timestamp and never removes entries. After months of traffic, this Map will contain an entry for every unique email that ever used the endpoint. On a server with thousands of lead magnet users, this is an unbounded memory leak.

Fix: Add a periodic cleanup interval (e.g., every hour, delete entries older than 24h) or use the same filter-on-read pattern as the IP tracker. Example:
```ts
setInterval(() => {
  const cutoff = Date.now() - DAY_MS;
  for (const [email, ts] of dailyEmailTracker) {
    if (ts < cutoff) dailyEmailTracker.delete(email);
  }
}, 60 * 60 * 1000); // hourly
```

🔴 **Bug**: `updateAssignment` accepts a `status` field in the updates but does NOT validate it against `VALID_STATUSES`. A caller could set `status: 'banana'` via update and it would be written to DynamoDB — then the `RunnerIndex` GSI (which partitions on `status`) would silently store items under an invalid partition key, and `getAssignmentsReadyToRun()` (which queries `status = 'active'`) would never find them.

Fix: Add validation at the top of `updateAssignment`:
```ts
if (updates.status !== undefined && !VALID_STATUSES.includes(updates.status)) {
  throw new Error(`Invalid status: ${updates.status}. Must be one of: ${VALID_STATUSES.join(', ')}`);
}
```

Also add a test case for this — the test file covers `createAssignment` validation but not `updateAssignment` status validation.

🔴 **Bug**: No status guard before promoting. If `approval.status` is already `"approved"`, calling this endpoint again will trigger a second `promoteToProduction()` call to Vercel. Add an idempotency check:

```ts
if (approval.status !== 'pending') {
  return res.status(409).json({ message: 'Approval already processed' });
}
```

Same issue exists in `/reject` (line 165) — a rejected deployment can be rejected again. And the Slack `approve_landing_page` handler in `slack.ts` also lacks this guard.

🔴 **Bug**: No authorization check. Any authenticated user can approve or reject any landing page deployment. The existing asset approval flow goes through Slack interactions (where the approver is inherently authorized via Slack channel membership), but this REST endpoint is wide open.

At minimum, add an `isAdmin` middleware check on `/approve` and `/reject`, or verify the user is the original requester or an admin:

```ts
router.post('/approve', isAdmin, async (req, res) => { ... });
router.post('/reject', isAdmin, async (req, res) => { ... });
```

_🔒 Security & Privacy_ | _🟠 Major_ | _⚡ Quick win_

**Traverse arrays during redaction.**

`walk` only descends into plain objects. It returns arrays unchanged. A dimension such as `{ recommendations: [{ estimatedSavingsMicroUsd: 4000000 }] }` reaches the sink with the internal value intact.

Recurse through array elements before writing the dimension. Add a regression test for nested arrays.



<details>
<summary>Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
+  const walkValue = (value: unknown): unknown => {
+    if (Array.isArray(value)) return value.map(walkValue);
+    return isPlainObject(value) ? walk(value) : value;
+  };
+
   const walk = (input: TelemetryDimensions): TelemetryDimensions => {
     const out: TelemetryDimensions = {};
     for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(input)) {
       if (MONEY_KEY.test(key) && !MONEY_KEY_EXEMPT.test(key)) {
         removed.push(key);
         continue;
       }
-      out[key] = isPlainObject(value) ? walk(value) : value;
+      out[key] = walkValue(value);
     }
     return out;
   };
```
</details>

